WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.640
Back in June at AI

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:05.480
DevCon in London, we had a set of talks
that all pointed the same way.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.240
If the specification is the thing
you actually maintain,

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.520
then the code becomes an output.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.400
Something you can throw away
and generate again.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.480
let's first hear from Simon Martinelli,
a consultant in Switzerland

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.760
who does this on real enterprise systems

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.520
and has a lovely way
of describing the new shape of the work.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:26.320
Two weeks of specs.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.600
Five minutes of generating.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.080
And something that I didn't talk about

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.880
is in my opinion,
specs are very sustainable

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.800
or hopefully they will be sustainable
in the future because currently

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.040
what I'm doing is reverse
engineering of existing code into specs.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.480
And if we have specs,
we can probably generate

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.640
the same application in different
technology with a different UI.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.400
Maybe we don't have even a UI,
we have chat or something directly

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.320
from the specs and the business people

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:02.000
can directly change the way the systems
should behave without developer,

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:07.000
so they don't need developers
talking about that or looking into that.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.520
And this accelerates development.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:11.400
The problem is at the moment.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.880
It just accelerates development.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:16.000
Right.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.480
So the requirements phase
and the spec phase takes some time.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.600
For example I'm working
for this government for the Parliament.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.680
They are have a business case management
software that we want to modernize.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.200
And I'm the only one during the proof
of concept doing something with code.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.000
But we have two product only requirements
engineers that work on the spec,

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:42.600
and they have much more work to do
than I have, I trust automate everything.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:46.640
So currently I'm working on on a pipeline
that they can

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:50.560
just change the model files and everything
will automatically generate it.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:54.840
So the kind of the work moves
or shifts left,

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.560
so everything shifts left to requirements
engineering in my opinion,

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.800
because now that's obvious
before we add like

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.360
if it did scrum, you had two weeks
to work on the requirements somehow.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:10.000
And then we are two weeks
working on the implementation.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.000
Now you have two weeks

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.320
and then five minutes and two weeks,
something like that.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:14.920
Right.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:19.320
We also heard from Dave Farley,
who takes the idea further than most.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:22.720
A program used to be a precise solution,
with the problem

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:24.920
left implicit in our heads.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:26.680
He thinks it becomes a precise

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:30.320
description of the problem,
and the AI does the translating.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:35.200
So how should programing change
to keep up with all of this?

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:37.560
So problem

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:41.320
one specifying what we want
with precision.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:45.080
In the past,
the way that we did this was that a

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:49.480
program was a precise solution
encoded as algorithms.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.920
Weirdly,
we kind of lost what the problem was.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.720
It's kind of implicit in our solution,
but we'd keep that in our heads

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:01.880
and we'd build a solution,
and that would be a definition

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:04.880
that we'd work to.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.480
But the solution itself was implicit.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:13.920
So in this in my example,
this is a routing algorithm.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.920
So I'd like to find my way
home is the outcome

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.920
that I'm trying to achieve here.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.440
In the future

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:31.520
a program will be a precise description
of what it is that we want,

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:37.200
I think encoded as specifications,
translated into executable instructions

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:41.400
by the AI that will verify
that we got what we wanted

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:47.080
and what we want now is explicitly part of
if you like the program.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:53.920
So the program moves away
from being solution focus to being

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.840
a more accurate description of the problem
that we're trying to solve.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:01.800
I'm describing
a form of spec-driven development,

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:06.960
but the specification takes the form
of executable specifications tests.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:12.880
If you like that, both specify what
we want and also verify that we got it.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.600
What it takes to achieve that program.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:24.840
I've just just repeating what I said
designing a domain specific language,

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:26.480
if you like, for specifying

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:30.240
what it is that we want from our software
with a deal of precision

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:35.600
and then specifying what we aim
to achieve in detail all of the time.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:38.880
And those those are all of the behaviors
of the system,

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:43.600
not just the conventional user behaviors
that we might think about.

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:44.560
We're not talking about

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:48.160
sketchy testing of the system,
but all of the behaviors of the system.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:50.760
So how fast would we like
the results back?

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:53.760
If performance matters,
how secure do we want it to be?

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.200
If we want secure our system to be secure?

00:04:56.200 --> 00:05:00.240
Because all of those are contextual,
you can't have the AI to infer that

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:03.240
to make it up, because it depends

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.280
the real how security
you want your system to be.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.440
You'd be stupid to apply the level

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:13.400
of security to a single user game.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:14.000
Then you would.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:17.000
If you were building a system for a bank,
because you'd be over

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:20.360
engineering the game
and probably under engineering the bank.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:25.760
So you need to be specific about what
it is that you're trying to achieve

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:31.320
And to close, Shachar Azriel of Baz, answering
a good question from the audience.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:34.760
Why point an agent at the spec
rather than have it right?

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:35.600
Tests.

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:38.600
And there is a lovely side effect
in his answer.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:41.560
Once teams
new an agent would read their specs,

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:44.000
they started writing better specs.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:49.480
The problem that I have with tests is that
they test things that nobody really

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:51.040
like.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:53.320
They're not testing real life.

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:57.080
Like teams are sitting and thinking
about these imaginary scenarios

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:00.760
that are not going to be not
going to happen any time in your product.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:05.800
And there are plenty of AI code
coding tools that generate tests

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:10.480
like here,
take like 100, 200, 500 unit tests.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:14.600
And in the end of the day, there is one
scenario that you're not thinking about.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.800
The idea
we chose this specific approach is

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.400
we wanted to leverage data
that is already there.

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:25.600
Like, I don't want someone to think
about scenarios

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:29.360
that that should that might happen
or might not.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:31.800
And let's use the specs to do that.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:36.000
By the way, the byproduct of this process,
and I didn't talk about it,

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:39.160
but I heard about it
from a lot of our customers,

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:42.760
is the fact
that when they knew that the specs are

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:48.400
used to be for the to the agents
to verify the feature,

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:51.680
it encouraged them to write better specs.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:55.040
So it's like renewable energy, right?

00:06:55.040 --> 00:07:00.600
Like, I'm bringing a new tool
that improves best practices and improves

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:02.960
and encourages
product managers and designers

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:05.680
to be more specific
in the way they write specs.

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:08.680
And I think that, like if testing

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:11.840
was the answer,
there wasn't room for this.

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:13.160
Thanks for watching.

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:17.840
Be sure to join us for the next
AI DevCon this November in New York City.

